    "problem": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "band",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "The problem",
        "heading": "Four ways a walk gets your feet wet.",
        "lede": "<p>Only one of these is the one boots are designed to stop, which is why good boots and wet feet are not a contradiction.</p>",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Over the top",
            "body": "<p>Wet grass, bracken and heather put water onto the boot from above, where the gusset and the laces are. Gaiters help. They do not seal.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The boot wets out",
            "body": "<p>The outer fabric saturates, the membrane can no longer pass vapour, and the boot stops breathing. It is not leaking — it is full. This is the four-hour problem.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Stream crossings and bog",
            "body": "<p>There is a depth beyond which no footwear helps, and on wet British ground you meet it more often than the forecast suggests.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "From the inside",
            "body": "<p>Eight hours of walking produces a lot of vapour. Some of it condenses. By the afternoon, plenty of dry-footed walkers are damp anyway.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3",
        "i4"
      ]
    },
    "answers": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "ink",
        "layout": "boxes",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "The answer",
        "heading": "A layer that does not wet out.",
        "lede": "<p>The membrane sits inside the sock rather than inside the boot, which changes where the waterproof line is drawn — and what happens when the boot fails.</p>",
        "link_label": "How the three layers work",
        "link_url": "/pages/technology",
        "mirror": false
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The boot can be soaked and the foot dry",
            "body": "<p>Once the waterproof layer is on the foot, a saturated boot is uncomfortable rather than decisive. You stop planning the route around the puddles.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "It works with any boot, including the wrong one",
            "body": "<p>Most people discover waterproof socks because their boots have started leaking. It is the cheapest useful thing to try before spending two hundred pounds.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "It is not a boot replacement",
            "body": "<p>Said plainly here and on the homepage: if your footwear is wrong for the ground, this will not fix it. It solves wetness, not support, grip or ankle protection.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
